-- The DATA-MODEL exhibit for the DMN exporter (Phase 3 of
-- `specs/todo/DMN-EXPORT-PROGRAM-MODEL-SPEC.md`).
--
-- The other two DMN goldens are about DECISION SHAPE. This one is about TYPES,
-- and it exists because §10's Phase 3 row and §4.2.1-8 both demand a golden that
-- pins the `Lossy` nullary-only arm of `D-SUMTYPE` — the case where the emitted
-- table is CORRECT (measured in KIE 8.44, §4.2.1-2) while its DOMAIN is not.
-- Everything else here is a neighbouring case that has to be told apart from it,
-- so a reader can see the line R4 and R8 actually draw:
--
--   * `Disposal`               a payload-carrying IS ONE OF. Its itemDefinition
--                              lists ALL THREE constructors in <allowedValues>,
--                              including the payload one, because that is what
--                              makes the missing rule visible to a gap analyser
--                              as a gap rather than as silence;
--   * `is a sale`              CONSIDERs it over its NULLARY arms only, with an
--                              OTHERWISE. The table survives, carries
--                              <inputValues>, and the report says which
--                              constructor has no cell     [D-SUMTYPE, lossy];
--   * `stated term`            projects the payload field. FEEL has no tagged
--                              value, so this is refused  [D-SUMTYPE, blocking];
--   * `claim amount`           merely THREADS a record with a sum-typed field,
--                              never reading it            [D-SUMTYPE, lossy];
--   * `capped at ten`          GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER, constructing JUST/NOTHING.
--                              Refused, and half the point of the fixture is
--                              that `NOTHING` appears NOWHERE as a FEEL string
--                              constant  [D-SUMTYPE blocking, D-MAYBE-NULL lossy];
--   * `grade is settled`       takes a MAYBE over a type that HAS a domain, so
--                              `q` shows R8-a's CARVE-OUT: its typeRef is
--                              `Grade_optional`, a minted alias over `string`
--                              with NO allowedValues, not `Grade`. Pointing at
--                              `Grade` would re-assert the complete range one
--                              typeRef hop on -- 7.3.3 reads the range off
--                              whatever the typeRef resolves to -- and that
--                              range has no absent case, so an enforcing
--                              engine would reject the NOTHING. What the alias
--                              costs is the OTHER half of a domain's job: at
--                              `q` a value outside the three constructors is
--                              no longer rejected either  [D-MAYBE-NULL lossy];
--   * `Claim`                  a record with a MAYBE field, a LIST OF field and
--                              a COMPUTED field  [D-MAYBE-NULL, D-ITEMDEF lossy];
--   * `deep`                   GIVEN a MAYBE (MAYBE NUMBER). `null` does not
--                              nest, so JUST NOTHING and NOTHING would become
--                              one FEEL value and FEEL `=` would answer TRUE
--                              where L4 answers FALSE      [D-SUMTYPE blocking];
--   * `Grade` / `grade for`    the control: a plain nullary IS ONE OF, whose
--                              table is inside the analysable fragment and
--                              carries no note at all;
--   * `number` / `is alpha`    a type name that collides with a FEEL BUILT-IN
--                              type spelling. A typeRef is resolved against the
--                              built-in names, so an itemDefinition called
--                              `number` would not shadow FEEL's numeric type but
--                              ALIAS onto it, and `n` (a genuine NUMBER) and `k`
--                              (this enum) would share one typeRef with nothing
--                              in the artifact saying they differ. The type name
--                              is suffixed instead, so read `number_` here and
--                              `number` on `n`.
--
-- Two things to read off the artifact rather than from this list. First, the
-- alias is minted ONCE and only where it is pointed at: `Claim`'s `assessed
-- grade` and the inputData `q` both carry typeRef="Grade_optional" and there is
-- exactly one `itemdef_grade_optional`, while `Disposal` -- which has a domain
-- too, but is never reached through a MAYBE -- gets no alias at all. Second,
-- `capped at ten` is GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER and its variable stays
-- typeRef="number": a builtin asserts no range, so there is nothing for the
-- typeRef hop to re-assert and nothing an alias would buy.
--
-- And one more: `Claim`'s
-- COMPUTED field does not appear as an <itemComponent>, and it raises no note
-- either. That is not an omission — `L4.Desugar` rewrites a computed field into
-- a synthetic top-level DECIDE before type checking, so by the time the exporter
-- sees the DECLARE the field is already a decision of its own, reading a `_self`
-- inputData typed `Claim`. The information is relocated, not dropped.
--
-- Figures and terms are illustrative; the point is the TYPE, not the statute.

§ `Sum types and the data model`

DECLARE Grade
  IS ONE OF
    `high`
    `low`

DECLARE Disposal
  IS ONE OF
    `sell`
    `lease` HAS `term in years` IS A NUMBER
    `assign`

-- A record whose fields exercise D-ITEMDEF's collection reason, R8-b's domain
-- suppression, the sum-typed component R4-a rules Lossy at the decision that
-- threads it, and the computed field the desugarer has already taken away.
DECLARE Claim
  HAS `amount`           IS A NUMBER
      `assessed grade`   IS A MAYBE Grade
      `supporting notes` IS A LIST OF STRING
      `the disposal`     IS A Disposal
      `doubled amount`   IS A NUMBER MEANS `amount` TIMES 2

-- The `Lossy` nullary-only arm. Two of the three constructors have a cell; the
-- third carries a payload and cannot have one. The table is still exactly what
-- the L4 says, which is why this is a domain loss and not a refusal.
GIVEN d IS A Disposal
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a sale` d IF
  CONSIDER d
  WHEN `sell`   THEN TRUE
  WHEN `assign` THEN FALSE
  OTHERWISE FALSE

-- The `Blocking` arm: reading the payload itself.
GIVEN disposal IS A Disposal
GIVETH A NUMBER
`stated term` disposal MEANS
  disposal's `term in years`

-- Threading, not reading. Nothing here touches the sum type, so the table is
-- fine; what is lost is the component's type, which FEEL cannot carry.
GIVEN c IS A Claim
GIVETH A NUMBER
`claim amount` c MEANS
  c's `amount`

-- R8-d. A total function whose value channel carries the absence — which is
-- exactly why §2.4 does NOT refuse it — but whose VALUES have no FEEL spelling.
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`capped at ten` n MEANS
  IF n AT MOST 10 THEN JUST n ELSE NOTHING

-- R8-a and R8-b at an inputData: `q` is emitted as typeRef="Grade", and Grade's
-- domain is NOT emitted, because `null` cannot be listed in it.
GIVEN q IS A MAYBE Grade
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`grade is settled` q MEANS
  CONSIDER q
  WHEN JUST g  THEN TRUE
  WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE

-- R8-c. `null` does not nest.
GIVEN m IS A MAYBE (MAYBE NUMBER)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`deep` m MEANS
  TRUE

-- The control. A plain nullary IS ONE OF: a U table, constant outputs, an
-- itemDefinition with a complete domain, and nothing lost.
GIVEN g IS A Grade
GIVETH A NUMBER
`grade for` g MEANS
  CONSIDER g
  WHEN `high` THEN 2
  WHEN `low`  THEN 1

-- The built-in type-name collision. Read the emitted itemDefinition name and
-- the two typeRefs together: `k` is typeRef="number_" and `n`, above, is
-- typeRef="number". Without the suffix both would read "number", and a consumer
-- resolving `n`'s type would land on this string enum.
DECLARE `number`
  IS ONE OF
    `alpha`
    `beta`

GIVEN k IS A `number`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`is alpha` k MEANS
  CONSIDER k
  WHEN `alpha` THEN TRUE
  WHEN `beta`  THEN FALSE
